I think it depends on your model architecture. In general, you should have a separate BatchNorm layer for each convolutional layer. This way, the normalization is done per channel, which makes sense because you're normalizing activations of the same filter across all spatial locations. You could do something like:

```
class ConvBlock(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, in_channels, out_channels):
        super(ConvBlock, self).__init__()
        self.conv = nn.Conv2d(in_channels, out_channels, kernel_size=3, padding=1)
        self.bn = nn.BatchNorm2d(out_channels)
        self.relu = nn.ReLU(inplace=True)

    def forward(self, x):
        return self.relu(self.bn(self.conv(x)))

class MyModel(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super(MyModel, self).__init__()
        self.block1 = ConvBlock(3, 64)
        self.block2 = ConvBlock(64, 128)
        # etc...
```

This way, you're applying batch normalization to the output of each convolutional layer.

I'm not sure why you're getting that error. However, you could try changing your `CustomImageDataset` to something like this:

```
class CustomImageDataset(Dataset):
    def __init__(self, annotations_file, img_dir, transform=None, target_transform = None):
        self.img_labels = pd.read_csv(annotations_file, sep= ';')
        self.img_dir = img_dir
        self.transform = transform
        self.target_transform = target_transform

    def __len__(self):
        return len(self.img_labels)

    def __getitem__(self, idx):
        img_path = os.path.join(self.img_dir, self.img_labels.iloc[idx, 0][0:9] + '.tar', self.img_labels.iloc[idx, 0])
        image = read_image(img_path)
        transf = transforms.Resize((224, 224))
        image = transf(image)
        label = self.img_labels.iloc[idx, 1]
        if self.transform:
            image = self.transform(image)
        if self.target_transform:
            label = self.target_transform(label)
        return image.unsqueeze(0), label
```

I've added `.unsqueeze(0)` to the `image` variable. This adds an additional dimension to the tensor, making it a single channel image.

The problem here is that you're passing a `std::vector` instead of a `torch::Tensor`. You can convert your `std::vector` to a `torch::Tensor` using [`torch::from_blob`](https://pytorch.org/cppdocs/api.html#_CPPv4N5torch10from_blobsEPKvPKlPKdPKfPKbPKhPKtPKcPKwPKjPKzPKPNS_4TypeENS_10MemoryFormatE):

```
// Create a vector of inputs.
std::vector<float> row = {0.160625, 0.967468297, 3.520480583, 0.862454481, -0.341933766};
std::vector<torch::Tensor> inputs;

inputs.push_back(torch::from_blob(&row[0], {1, 5}, torch::kFloat));

// Execute the model and turn its output into a tensor.
at::Tensor output = module.forward(inputs).toTensor();
std::cout << output.slice(/*dim=*/1, /*start=*/0, /*end=*/4) << '\n';
```
